@@ -155,7 +155,7 @@ Some features are not supported in AdvCiv-SAS; e.g., non-English languages, pre-
 - **Timestamped BBAI log files:** Optionally log each new game and loaded save to a **new UTC-timestamped file** (e.g., `BBAI_20260705T071718Z_new1.log` or `BBAI_20260705T071718Z_load2.log`) instead of expanding the existing `BBAI.log`, which was very tedious to clean up or identify/store/read/review/upload, and so repeated save-file tests no longer require restarting Civ4. Each log begins notably with the new/load time, currently active BBAI log levels, turn/year, map and game settings (including `landheavy` and `navalheavy` actual game information), player counts, and RNG states, avoiding mixed logs and the need to restart Civ4 between repeated save-file tests. See also [README.md (BBAI logging and head example)](/README.md#bbai-logging-and-head-example).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6022+) **SAS game-summary log:** added a separate `SASGameSummary_*.log` for compact autoplay and AI-strength review. It records high-level run context such as economy, expansion, city and battle history, worked plots, diplomacy, unit composition, and game state, complementing BBAI logs by summarizing what happened before or alongside detailed AI-decision traces. Note: it currently contains spoilers: a spoiler-free implementation was considered, so the human/active player can give to an LLM or such their play summary and get LLM or such suggestions or feedback/analysis, but it would require an extensive rework, so currently this is not supported; therefore, SAS game-summary log should as of now not be treated as a spoiler-free player-advice export. Note 2: depending on `SAS_GAME_SUMMARY_TURN_INTERVAL` value, the last turn may not appear in the log: for example, an autoplay run of 200 turns ends at T200 and thus does not seem to show the T200 data, only up to T190, so autoplay at least 201 turns to show T200 for example if you have this issue. See also [README.md (DLL Logging)](/README.md#dll-logging).
 - **BBAI worker log split:** added separate worker and Work Boat / `UNITAI_WORKER_SEA` BBAI log-level defines so ordinary `SAS_BBAI_UNIT_LOG_LEVEL` can focus on non-worker unit behavior such as scrapping, army movement, and combat-unit decisions. Worker and worker-sea logs had become noisy enough to hide those unit decisions, and Work Boats use distinct logic from land workers, so they now have their own category as well.
-- **BBAI Settler log split:** added `SAS_BBAI_SETTLER_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT so Settlers and other units can be inspected independently. This notably covers city-site decisions and Settler movement, scouting, returns, and founding; FOUND as of now separately controls detailed city-site scoring calculations. This notably lets UNIT investigate other unit behavior without enabling the multi-megabyte first-city Settler traces needed for example for the Berlin, Karakorum, and Cuzco regression tests.
+- **BBAI Settler log split:** added `SAS_BBAI_SETTLER_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT so Settlers and other units can be inspected independently. This notably covers city-site decisions, Settler movement, scouting, returns, founding, (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6027+) parking reasons, danger context, and city escort pools; FOUND as of now separately controls detailed city-site scoring calculations. This notably lets UNIT investigate other unit behavior without enabling the multi-megabyte first-city Settler traces needed for example for the Berlin, Karakorum, and Cuzco regression tests.
 - **Culture-victory BBAI diagnostics:** added a new dedicated `SAS_BBAI_CULTURE_LOG_LEVEL` notably for diagnosing premature, uneven, or wasteful AI culture investment without enabling broad player/city logs. It records for example culture-stage reasons, current/projected Legendary-city countdowns and rival ranking, competing victory progress, actual culture-slider decisions, per-city weights and production, and culture contributed by production processes and assigned specialists.
 - **Citizen-allocation BBAI diagnostics:** added a dedicated `SAS_BBAI_CITIZEN_LOG_LEVEL` notably for finding worked plots or specialists that the AI's full city-aware comparison would replace, but its faster preliminary selection overlooked. At the highest level, it also reports suspicious worked/unworked plot pairs under a simple food-first yield check.
 - **Evacuation and retreat BBAI diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_EVACUATION_LOG_LEVEL`. It notably helps investigate evacuation/retreat behavior without broad city or unit logging, for doomed cities, workers, and naval units.
@@ -328,7 +328,8 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - **First-city roaming**: Since the first city is especially important, unlike later cities that can be more strategic or semi-decent resource grabs, starting settlers can now **roam briefly for a better first-city site when the current site is bad enough**. Settlers can spend the bounded early window to **move to visible good-enough sites**, do **safe adjacent scouting for better information**, or **wait if no safe scouting step exists**. Starting-site checks use a shared **potential-food environment score** (using the same logic described under Low-food environments). Food-surplus plots offset poor plots, productive Grass Hills remain neutral, and no-yield peaks/ice receive an extra citizen-unworkable penalty because they are dead BFC slots. This keeps useful resources/coasts from looking poor while still moving away from bad tundra/plains/peak-heavy starts.
 - **First-city recheck and efficient return after scouting:** After leaving a very poor spawn location, the AI settler explores nearby unrevealed plots (fog) within a limited scouting window (in turns) instead of founding the first merely good-enough site, trying to find better first-city sites, like a human player would. Only fully revealed BFCs can interrupt scouting; before each further step, the AI settler protects the strongest newly discovered reachable site by full city-site value, with the tunable return-path cost breaking exact ties. If the raw-value winner is instead the poor scout origin it deliberately left, sites are compared after subtracting their return-path cost so a nearby partly revealed candidate can finish being explored instead of needlessly undoing the scouting trip. The turns window includes the trip back, so the AI settler commits before further scouting would make its strongest return too late, then queues movement and founding together instead of restarting scouting on arrival or founding wherever scouting stopped. Once this window has ended, return-path cost can still favor a nearly equal nearby site over lengthy backtracking. Empirically and as BBAI logging also showed, this helped Berlin, Karakorum, Cuzco, and the Aztecs choose strong revealed sites and found promptly in save files 442, 360, and 431. As of now this applies only to the first city, which critically benefits from extra information and founds much stronger first-city sites, while later AI settlers usually know more of the local map and so do not need to use this (so as of now disabled for them). See [KI#144](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#144---fixed-base-advciv-issue-and-advciv-sas-settler-free-window-follow-up-ai-settlers-sometimes-do-not-move-away-from-a-high-bad-plot-count-start-eg-high-non-bonus-tundra-and-plains-they-now-scout-and-hunt-for-better-not-very-bad-sites-and-no-longer-stop-at-first-good-enough-site-but-instead-now-rerun-evaluate-city-site-again-on-newly-visible-plots-if-a-better-site-eg-more-foodriversfresh-water-exists-nearby-which-we-now-value-more-too-for-first-city-as-well).
 - **Optional naval-heavy-map coastal settling bias:** Naval-heavy maps can now add a tunable first-city or later-city preference for coastal settling in naval-heavy maps. This should be more useful and strategically stronger in such maps, notably for coastal access, work boats, scouting/contact, trade, first galleys, Moai, or other water-focused strategies. However, following the [KI#173](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#173---fixedimproved-base-advciv-issue-live-first-settler-scoring-reused-hidden-map-generation-surroundings) and related AI settler/found scoring changes, BBAI logging showed even a value of `0` correctly selects the strong Cuzco coastal site in save file 420, while Japan no longer favors the old tundra-heavy candidates that motivated this new feature. So use only a fairly small naval settling bias positive value for additional strategic coastal preference. See [KI#146](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#146---fixedenhanced-base-advciv-issue-of-ai-undervaluing-coastal-settling-on-naval-heavy-maps).
-- **Parking addressed (early game):** Reduced cases of settlers idling in the capital despite safe/urgent expansion, by updating `CvUnitAI::AI_found` to found sooner when conditions allow. In autoplay samples, Spain shifted from **1 city @T100** to **~2 by T60–70** and **~3 by T80–90** (settler production varies somehow, but we move much sooner after we have it now). See [KI#68](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#68---tremendously-improved-ai-having-a-settler-parked-from-turn---45-to-turn-100-and-still-didnt-found-any-city-fixedaddressed-in-cvunitaiai_found).
+- **Parking addressed (early game):** Reduced cases of Settlers idling in the capital despite safe/urgent expansion, by updating `CvUnitAI::AI_found` so AIs would found sooner when conditions allowed. In autoplay samples, Spain shifted from **1 city @T100** to **~2 by T60–70** and **~3 by T80–90** (Settler production varies somehow, but we move much sooner after we have it now). Update: however, (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6027+) save file 450 BBAI testing showed that this old blind "go settle anyway" safety override solved one-city stagnation too bluntly: it could also allow exposed post-capital cities to be founded without escorts and then razed. Exposed post-capital Settlers now wait, retreat, or require an existing guard-city mission instead of leaving/founding alone. See [KI#68](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#68---tremendously-improved-ai-having-a-settler-parked-from-turn---45-to-turn-100-and-still-didnt-found-any-city-fixedaddressed-in-cvunitaiai_found) and [KI#179](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#179---fixedimproved-advciv-sas-early-settler-anti-parking-override-could-let-post-capital-settlers-found-exposed-cities-without-escorts).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6027+) **Settler city-escort reassignment:** to avoid merely restoring old Settler parking, an unescorted Settler parked in one of its own cities can reassign one healthy local defender as escort when the donor city keeps enough healthy defenders behind. With the default tunable value of as of now at least 2 remaining healthy defenders after giving one to the Settler, a city with 3 healthy defenders can reassign 1 escort and still keep 2, which is safe enough, so the Settler should not keep waiting; delaying expansion can lose contested city sites and early growth snowball. Save file 450 follow-up logs showed exposed Settlers waiting when no safe escort was available, then moving once guarded, while later Nobamba persisted with defenders instead of repeating the earlier empty-city Barbarian razing pattern. See [KI#179](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#179---fixedimproved-advciv-sas-early-settler-anti-parking-override-could-let-post-capital-settlers-found-exposed-cities-without-escorts).
 
